WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.400
- We are launching a whole new channel.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.710
There have been video
ideas that I've pitched

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.380
that don't fully belong on "Veritasium."

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.850
- I've been in those pitch meetings.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:09.683
I've seen those.

00:00:09.683 --> 00:00:11.520
I've seen that happen.
- But that doesn't mean

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:12.870
that the videos shouldn't exist

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:13.890
and that they aren't

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:15.360
saying something very interesting.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.520
We need another way to tell these stories.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.910
- We at "Veritasium," we have a lot

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.460
of exciting stories that we want to tell.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.080
Same Veritasium rigour,

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.240
but this time it's about social science.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.000
It's about how our complex

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.970
and constantly changing world works.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:34.140
So that's what we're doing.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.440
We're launching a whole new
channel called "InGenius."

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.600
You can watch the first few minutes here,

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.457
and if you like it, come to our channel,

00:00:42.457 --> 00:00:44.407
"InGenius," and you can watch the rest.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:50.870
This $40 hunk of metal
rewired the modern world.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.210
It started a revolution

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.820
that transformed China from being one

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.860
of the poorest countries in the world,

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:00.690
to a global superpower,

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:04.560
controlling 30% of all manufacturing.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.140
It's a story that's
never been told before.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:10.140
How it inspires former spy, Yuan Geng,

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.450
to create an island of capitalism

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:15.000
in his home county with an invention

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.400
that changes the face of communist China,

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.350
spreading its influence across the world

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:23.610
and upending the entire global economy.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:26.643
But the story didn't start in China,

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:31.200
it starts with a
frustrated American trucker

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.069
from North Carolina.

00:01:33.069 --> 00:01:34.080
(soft blues music)

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:34.913
In 1937,

00:01:35.837 --> 00:01:39.570
24-year-old Malcolm McLean
is driving his truck full

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.150
of cotton bales from North Carolina

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.520
up to Hoboken, New Jersey.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.460
At port, he waits for
his truck to be unloaded,

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:51.060
and waits, and waits.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.910
It's painstakingly slow,
almost a whole day,

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.520
and that's just his one truck.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:01.290
He figures, what if you took
the entire back of the truck

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.450
and just put it directly on the ship?

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.400
The whole thing would be over in a matter

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.020
of minutes instead of hours.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:09.240
- So why don't we put that
whole body on the truck?

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:10.887
That's my first thought, you know?

00:02:10.887 --> 00:02:11.940
- The problem is,

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:15.960
truck drivers can't control
how ships are loaded.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:20.070
So while McLean builds a highly
successful trucking empire

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:23.160
over the next 20 years,
he can only stand by

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:27.153
and watch as the cargo issue gets worse.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.670
This is the log from a 1954
cargo ship, the SS Warrior.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:37.380
194,582 total things on the ship

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.510
that would all have to be loaded

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:43.230
and unloaded by hand, by
gangs of longshoremen.

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:44.430
It takes so long.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.230
It means if you're shipping something,

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.510
you're paying twice as much for your cargo

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:52.920
to be stuck at port than
actually travelling on a ship.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.853
And it makes your cargo
vulnerable to another problem.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.120
The New York and New Jersey
waterfronts in the 50's

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:04.560
are dominated by the Genovese
and Gambino mafia families.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.380
Corruption, racketeering
and theft are endemic.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:10.440
More than $13 million
of cargo is estimated

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:11.822
to have been stolen just

00:03:11.822 --> 00:03:14.880
from those ports over a three-year period.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.720
That's more than 180
million in today's money.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.310
The upshot is,

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.470
the process is so expensive

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:24.540
that in most cases selling internationally

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.190
just isn't worth it.

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:29.430
So most consumer products stay local,

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:31.800
make locally, sell locally.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:35.760
So in 1955, McLane decides to act.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.470
He's convinced his old idea

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:40.410
of detachable truck
trailers is the solution.

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:44.250
So McLean does something drastic.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:47.100
He sells his 20-year-old trucking empire

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.910
and gambles everything on
an ocean shipping company,

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:54.390
despite the fact that he has
zero experience in the field.

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:57.600
But to turn his idea into
reality, he needs someone

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.290
who can actually build the
container of his dreams.

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:04.770
He hears about 35-year-old
engineer, Keith Tantlinger,

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:06.660
based out in Spokane, Washington,

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:09.120
who has built a transportable metal box

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.910
and even been tinkering
with trying to stack it.

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:14.520
Tantlinger's reasoning
is similar to McLean.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.070
Rather than waste precious ship space

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:21.401
with the truck or the wheels,
just transport the cargo

00:04:21.401 --> 00:04:26.401
in the box from the truck to
the ship in one seamless move.

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:30.510
McLean flies Tantlinger down
to his headquarters in Alabama

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:32.910
and immediately gets him
working on containers

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:36.360
that will fit onto a
converted World War II

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.070
oil tanker that he just bought.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.670
McLean renames the ship the SS Ideal X,

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:44.550
ideal for the ideal form of transportation

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:47.430
and X for experimentation.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.250
- It was the SpaceX of the 1950's

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:54.513
and in many ways Malcolm McLean
is the Elon Musk of his day.

00:04:55.431 --> 00:04:57.848
(soft music)

